1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,220
1274 BC.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:03,670
Syria.

3
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:08,350
The most powerful man in the
world is about to be ambushed.

4
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,310
His name is Ramesses the Second.

5
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:11,830
Pharaoh of Egypt.

6
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:12,980
Son of the sun god.

7
00:00:13,030 --> 00:00:15,770
Twenty-five years old.

8
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:19,050
And very, very sure of himself.

9
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:23,690
In a few hours, his army will be cut
in half, his camp will be overrun,

10
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:26,950
and he will be fighting for his life.

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,410
And yet, when it was all over, he went
home and carved his great victory into the

12
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:34,970
walls of five temples.

13
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:37,010
Meanwhile, the other king went home...

14
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:40,210
and recorded his victory too.

15
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:43,920
So who actually won the Battle of Kadesh?

16
00:00:46,208 --> 00:00:47,958
Quick correction first.

17
00:00:48,008 --> 00:00:52,258
Last episode, we said this was
a battle between two pharaohs.

18
00:00:52,308 --> 00:00:53,138
It wasn't.

19
00:00:53,188 --> 00:00:55,318
Only one of them was a pharaoh.

20
00:00:55,368 --> 00:00:57,618
The other was a Hittite king.

21
00:00:57,668 --> 00:01:01,168
Which honestly makes
the story even better.

22
00:01:01,488 --> 00:01:06,318
Around 1300 BC, the Middle
East had two superpowers.

23
00:01:06,368 --> 00:01:08,138
In the south, Egypt.

24
00:01:08,188 --> 00:01:13,218
Rich, ancient, and ruled by god-kings
for well over a thousand years.

25
00:01:13,268 --> 00:01:16,118
In the north, the Hittites.

26
00:01:16,168 --> 00:01:19,258
An empire from the mountains
of what is now Turkey,

27
00:01:19,308 --> 00:01:23,478
with tough soldiers and some of
the best chariots in the world.

28
00:01:23,528 --> 00:01:27,658
And between them sat a strip
of land everyone wanted.

29
00:01:27,708 --> 00:01:29,418
Syria and Canaan.

30
00:01:29,468 --> 00:01:32,418
Trade routes, cities, and gold.

31
00:01:32,468 --> 00:01:35,498
Whoever held it ruled the region.

32
00:01:35,548 --> 00:01:40,458
The key to it all was a fortified
city on the Orontes River.

33
00:01:40,508 --> 00:01:43,128
Its name was Kadesh.

34
00:01:43,268 --> 00:01:47,638
Neither empire could ever let go of it.

35
00:01:47,688 --> 00:01:53,338
For more than a century, the little kingdoms
of Syria switched sides whenever the

36
00:01:53,388 --> 00:01:59,418
wind changed, paying tribute to Egypt
one year and to the Hittites the next.

37
00:01:59,468 --> 00:02:03,318
Kadesh itself had changed
sides more than once.

38
00:02:03,368 --> 00:02:06,358
And it was not an easy city to take.

39
00:02:06,408 --> 00:02:12,558
It sat between the Orontes and a smaller
stream, with a moat dug across the gap,

40
00:02:12,608 --> 00:02:16,998
so the city was almost
completely surrounded by water.

41
00:02:17,048 --> 00:02:20,992
If you held Kadesh, you
held the road north.

42
00:02:21,042 --> 00:02:24,262
Ramesses wanted Kadesh.

43
00:02:24,342 --> 00:02:28,492
His father had captured it
once, and lost it again.

44
00:02:28,542 --> 00:02:33,432
So in the fifth year of his reign, Ramesses
marched north with one of the biggest

45
00:02:33,482 --> 00:02:36,532
armies Egypt had ever assembled.

46
00:02:36,582 --> 00:02:39,632
Around twenty thousand men.

47
00:02:39,702 --> 00:02:44,172
Four divisions, each named after a god.

48
00:02:44,222 --> 00:02:45,272
Amun.

49
00:02:45,322 --> 00:02:46,482
Ra.

50
00:02:46,562 --> 00:02:47,752
Ptah.

51
00:02:47,802 --> 00:02:48,132
And Set.

52
00:02:48,182 --> 00:02:52,932
And the weapon of the age was the chariot.

53
00:02:52,982 --> 00:02:56,412
Egyptian chariots were light and fast.

54
00:02:56,462 --> 00:03:02,262
Two men: a driver and an archer,
shooting from a moving platform.

55
00:03:02,322 --> 00:03:05,152
Hittite chariots were heavier.

56
00:03:05,202 --> 00:03:09,812
Three men: a driver, a shield
bearer, and a spearman.

57
00:03:09,862 --> 00:03:12,752
Built to smash straight into the enemy.

58
00:03:12,802 --> 00:03:15,842
Think sports car against tank.

59
00:03:16,122 --> 00:03:21,612
Waiting for him was the Hittite
king, Muwatalli the Second.

60
00:03:21,662 --> 00:03:23,032
Older.

61
00:03:23,742 --> 00:03:24,772
Quieter.

62
00:03:24,822 --> 00:03:28,002
And much, much sneakier.

63
00:03:28,522 --> 00:03:32,972
And Muwatalli had every
reason to be ready.

64
00:03:33,022 --> 00:03:39,172
The year before, Ramesses had marched up
the coast and pulled the kingdom of Amurru,

65
00:03:39,222 --> 00:03:42,852
a Hittite ally, over to Egypt's side.

66
00:03:42,902 --> 00:03:46,492
For the Hittites, that
was a slap in the face.

67
00:03:46,542 --> 00:03:50,152
So Muwatalli called in
every favour he had.

68
00:03:50,202 --> 00:03:55,792
Allies came from all over Anatolia and
Syria, from more than a dozen different

69
00:03:55,842 --> 00:03:57,152
lands.

70
00:03:57,202 --> 00:04:02,572
Egyptian scribes claimed he brought
around two and a half thousand chariots.

71
00:04:02,622 --> 00:04:07,472
Whatever the real number, it was the biggest
army the Hittites had ever put in the

72
00:04:07,522 --> 00:04:07,852
field.

73
00:04:07,902 --> 00:04:12,533
And it was waiting for Ramesses at Kadesh.

74
00:04:12,583 --> 00:04:17,713
Near Kadesh, the Egyptians
met two local nomads.

75
00:04:17,763 --> 00:04:20,193
They said they wanted to switch sides.

76
00:04:20,243 --> 00:04:22,653
And they had great news.

77
00:04:22,703 --> 00:04:27,473
The Hittite army was far
away, up north near Aleppo.

78
00:04:27,523 --> 00:04:30,713
Too scared to face the mighty pharaoh.

79
00:04:30,923 --> 00:04:33,313
Ramesses loved this.

80
00:04:33,363 --> 00:04:34,873
He didn't check.

81
00:04:34,923 --> 00:04:40,593
He took his first division, Amun, and
raced ahead to build his camp right next to

82
00:04:40,643 --> 00:04:40,993
Kadesh.

83
00:04:41,043 --> 00:04:45,953
The rest of his army was strung
out for miles behind him.

84
00:04:46,003 --> 00:04:48,633
There was just one problem.

85
00:04:48,803 --> 00:04:52,333
The two nomads were Hittite spies.

86
00:04:52,683 --> 00:04:57,593
The entire Hittite army was hiding
on the other side of the city.

87
00:04:57,643 --> 00:04:59,193
Thousands of chariots.

88
00:04:59,243 --> 00:05:03,013
Close enough to see
the Egyptian campfires.

89
00:05:03,263 --> 00:05:06,433
Think about how close that was.

90
00:05:06,483 --> 00:05:11,273
Ramesses was sitting in his
new camp, feeling like a god.

91
00:05:11,323 --> 00:05:15,233
And just a short ride away, behind
the walls and across the river,

92
00:05:15,283 --> 00:05:18,573
thousands of horses were
waiting for a signal.

93
00:05:18,623 --> 00:05:23,113
Muwatalli didn't need to beat
the whole Egyptian army at once.

94
00:05:23,163 --> 00:05:26,173
He only needed to catch it in pieces.

95
00:05:26,223 --> 00:05:30,825
And thanks to his two spies,
that's exactly how it was arriving.

96
00:05:30,875 --> 00:05:36,615
Then Egyptian patrols caught
two real Hittite scouts.

97
00:05:36,755 --> 00:05:39,625
After a beating, they told the truth.

98
00:05:39,675 --> 00:05:42,345
The Hittites weren't in Aleppo.

99
00:05:42,395 --> 00:05:44,725
They were right there.

100
00:05:45,435 --> 00:05:46,875
Behind Kadesh.

101
00:05:47,075 --> 00:05:48,445
Ready.

102
00:05:49,175 --> 00:05:51,265
Ramesses was furious.

103
00:05:51,315 --> 00:05:55,925
He yelled at his officers and sent messengers
racing south to hurry the rest of the

104
00:05:55,975 --> 00:05:56,145
army.

105
00:05:56,195 --> 00:05:58,908
It was too late.

106
00:05:58,958 --> 00:06:05,488
The Hittite chariots crossed the river
and slammed into the second division, Ra,

107
00:06:05,538 --> 00:06:09,788
while it was still marching
in a long, loose column.

108
00:06:09,838 --> 00:06:11,748
Thousands of chariots.

109
00:06:11,798 --> 00:06:14,608
Each one carrying three men.

110
00:06:14,658 --> 00:06:17,048
The Ra division didn't form up.

111
00:06:17,098 --> 00:06:18,348
It didn't fight.

112
00:06:18,398 --> 00:06:20,388
It just broke.

113
00:06:20,938 --> 00:06:25,348
The survivors ran north,
straight into Ramesses' camp.

114
00:06:25,398 --> 00:06:28,428
With the Hittite chariots
right behind them.

115
00:06:28,478 --> 00:06:31,208
Now the camp was in chaos.

116
00:06:31,258 --> 00:06:32,808
Soldiers fleeing.

117
00:06:32,858 --> 00:06:34,368
Tents trampled.

118
00:06:34,418 --> 00:06:38,538
And the Pharaoh of Egypt
was nearly surrounded.

119
00:06:38,618 --> 00:06:40,868
Try to picture it.

120
00:06:40,918 --> 00:06:46,268
Wave after wave of heavy chariots,
each one pulled by two horses,

121
00:06:46,318 --> 00:06:52,728
pouring into a camp full of tents,
cattle, baggage and terrified men.

122
00:06:52,858 --> 00:06:54,388
Dust everywhere.

123
00:06:54,438 --> 00:06:58,188
Nobody knowing where the
next attack was coming from.

124
00:06:58,238 --> 00:07:04,468
For a few terrible minutes, the most
powerful army in the world had simply stopped

125
00:07:04,518 --> 00:07:06,700
being an army.

126
00:07:06,750 --> 00:07:10,040
Here's where the story gets interesting.

127
00:07:10,090 --> 00:07:14,260
According to Ramesses himself,
everyone abandoned him.

128
00:07:14,310 --> 00:07:20,180
So he prayed to the god Amun, climbed
into his chariot, and charged the enemy.

129
00:07:20,230 --> 00:07:21,360
Alone.

130
00:07:21,410 --> 00:07:23,240
Again and again.

131
00:07:23,290 --> 00:07:26,540
Defeating thousands with his own hands.

132
00:07:26,630 --> 00:07:28,740
That's his version.

133
00:07:28,790 --> 00:07:32,820
The temple carvings even show
his pet lion in the camp.

134
00:07:32,870 --> 00:07:34,740
Historians are a little skeptical.

135
00:07:34,790 --> 00:07:38,680
What probably happened is this.

136
00:07:38,730 --> 00:07:42,640
Ramesses really did fight
bravely with his bodyguard.

137
00:07:42,690 --> 00:07:46,220
And the Hittite charioteers
made a classic mistake.

138
00:07:46,270 --> 00:07:48,840
They stopped to loot the Egyptian camp.

139
00:07:48,890 --> 00:07:49,720
Gold.

140
00:07:49,770 --> 00:07:50,480
Food.

141
00:07:50,530 --> 00:07:51,500
Weapons.

142
00:07:51,550 --> 00:07:55,860
And then a fresh Egyptian
unit arrived from the coast.

143
00:07:55,910 --> 00:07:56,940
The Ne'arin.

144
00:07:56,990 --> 00:08:00,020
They hit the distracted
Hittites from the side.

145
00:08:00,070 --> 00:08:02,950
Then the Ptah division finally showed up.

146
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,740
Muwatalli sent in another
thousand chariots.

147
00:08:06,790 --> 00:08:11,000
But his huge army of foot
soldiers never crossed the river.

148
00:08:11,050 --> 00:08:14,930
By nightfall, the Hittite
chariots had pulled back.

149
00:08:15,110 --> 00:08:17,990
Ramesses had survived.

150
00:08:18,190 --> 00:08:18,980
Barely.

151
00:08:19,030 --> 00:08:23,700
And according to his own inscriptions,
the first thing he did afterwards was give

152
00:08:23,750 --> 00:08:26,240
his officers a furious speech.

153
00:08:26,290 --> 00:08:27,580
Where were you?

154
00:08:27,630 --> 00:08:28,400
I was alone!

155
00:08:28,450 --> 00:08:32,000
My horses did more than all of you!

156
00:08:32,050 --> 00:08:37,580
He even named the two horses that pulled
his chariot that day: Victory in Thebes,

157
00:08:37,630 --> 00:08:39,460
and Mut is Content.

158
00:08:39,510 --> 00:08:43,680
And he promised to feed them with
his own hands, every single day,

159
00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:45,180
for the rest of their lives.

160
00:08:45,230 --> 00:08:48,867
His officers, presumably, got nothing.

161
00:08:48,917 --> 00:08:52,787
The next day, the two armies fought again.

162
00:08:52,837 --> 00:08:54,487
Nobody broke through.

163
00:08:54,537 --> 00:09:00,647
Ramesses couldn't take Kadesh, and
his army was tired and far from home.

164
00:09:00,697 --> 00:09:04,687
So he turned around and
marched back to Egypt.

165
00:09:04,817 --> 00:09:06,827
Kadesh stayed Hittite.

166
00:09:06,877 --> 00:09:10,407
The Hittites even pushed
further south afterwards.

167
00:09:10,457 --> 00:09:14,307
On paper, that looks a
lot like a Hittite win.

168
00:09:14,557 --> 00:09:19,727
But back home, Ramesses had his version
carved into the walls of his greatest

169
00:09:19,777 --> 00:09:20,827
temples.

170
00:09:20,877 --> 00:09:21,827
Karnak.

171
00:09:21,877 --> 00:09:22,817
Luxor.

172
00:09:22,867 --> 00:09:23,807
Abydos.

173
00:09:23,857 --> 00:09:24,987
The Ramesseum.

174
00:09:25,037 --> 00:09:26,507
Abu Simbel.

175
00:09:26,557 --> 00:09:31,567
Huge scenes of him alone in his
chariot, crushing the Hittites.

176
00:09:31,617 --> 00:09:36,427
It might be the first big piece
of military propaganda in history.

177
00:09:36,477 --> 00:09:40,427
And in the Hittite records,
the story was very different.

178
00:09:40,477 --> 00:09:44,437
The Egyptian king had been
beaten, and sent home.

179
00:09:44,487 --> 00:09:48,007
For a long time, nobody could check.

180
00:09:48,057 --> 00:09:51,867
Historians had almost
forgotten the Hittites existed.

181
00:09:52,017 --> 00:09:57,317
Then, a little over a hundred years ago,
archaeologists dug up the Hittite capital,

182
00:09:57,367 --> 00:10:01,367
Hattusa, and found
thousands of clay tablets.

183
00:10:01,417 --> 00:10:04,657
Finally, we could hear the other side.

184
00:10:04,997 --> 00:10:06,207
Two kings.

185
00:10:06,257 --> 00:10:07,467
One battle.

186
00:10:07,517 --> 00:10:10,567
Two completely different stories.

187
00:10:10,817 --> 00:10:13,567
And the war didn't end there.

188
00:10:13,617 --> 00:10:18,887
For years after Kadesh, Egypt and the
Hittites kept fighting over the cities of

189
00:10:18,937 --> 00:10:24,607
Syria, raiding, besieging, and
taking towns back and forth.

190
00:10:24,657 --> 00:10:31,247
Ramesses came north again and again,
but nobody could land a knockout blow.

191
00:10:31,297 --> 00:10:34,307
Then Muwatalli died.

192
00:10:34,357 --> 00:10:41,247
His son became king, only to be pushed off
the throne by his own uncle, Hattusili.

193
00:10:41,297 --> 00:10:47,967
And far to the east, a new danger was
growing, one that worried both empires.

194
00:10:48,337 --> 00:10:49,533
Assyria.

195
00:10:49,583 --> 00:10:51,773
But here's the twist.

196
00:10:51,823 --> 00:10:57,713
About sixteen years later, Egypt and the
Hittites stopped fighting and signed a

197
00:10:57,763 --> 00:10:58,833
peace treaty.

198
00:10:58,883 --> 00:11:02,293
One of the oldest peace
treaties ever found.

199
00:11:02,343 --> 00:11:06,513
They promised not to attack each other,
to help each other against enemies,

200
00:11:06,563 --> 00:11:09,293
and to send back each other's runaways.

201
00:11:09,343 --> 00:11:14,233
The Egyptian copy was carved
in hieroglyphs on temple walls.

202
00:11:14,283 --> 00:11:18,653
The Hittite copy was written
on clay tablets in Hattusa.

203
00:11:18,703 --> 00:11:23,413
Archaeologists found both, and
they match almost line by line.

204
00:11:23,463 --> 00:11:25,933
Except for one detail.

205
00:11:25,983 --> 00:11:29,933
Each side says the other
side asked for peace first.

206
00:11:30,203 --> 00:11:33,973
Ramesses even married a Hittite princess.

207
00:11:34,023 --> 00:11:39,653
Today, a copy of that treaty is on
display at the United Nations in New York.

208
00:11:39,703 --> 00:11:44,413
The oldest peace deal, in the
home of modern peace deals.

209
00:11:44,763 --> 00:11:48,383
And the friendship got
surprisingly personal.

210
00:11:48,703 --> 00:11:53,293
The Egyptian queen, Nefertari,
and the Hittite queen, Puduhepa,

211
00:11:53,343 --> 00:11:56,733
wrote letters to each
other and exchanged gifts.

212
00:11:57,123 --> 00:12:02,013
Two royal families that had spent
decades trying to destroy each other,

213
00:12:02,063 --> 00:12:06,533
now sending each other jewellery and
asking about each other's health.

214
00:12:06,583 --> 00:12:10,013
So who won the Battle of Kadesh?

215
00:12:10,063 --> 00:12:13,393
On the battlefield, probably nobody.

216
00:12:13,443 --> 00:12:16,983
Ramesses survived a trap that
should have destroyed him.

217
00:12:17,033 --> 00:12:19,453
Muwatalli kept Kadesh.

218
00:12:19,503 --> 00:12:24,153
And in the end, both of them
decided the war wasn't worth it.

219
00:12:24,203 --> 00:12:27,273
But Ramesses won something else.

220
00:12:27,323 --> 00:12:31,973
More than three thousand years
later, we still know his name,

221
00:12:32,023 --> 00:12:34,653
and we still read his version.

222
00:12:34,703 --> 00:12:37,073
He didn't just fight the battle.

223
00:12:37,123 --> 00:12:39,533
He won the story.

224
00:12:39,963 --> 00:12:42,133
Next time: Assyria.

225
00:12:42,183 --> 00:12:48,233
The most feared empire on Earth, and
how it disappeared in just a few years.

226
00:12:48,283 --> 00:12:52,303
Subscribe to Spears and
Tears, so you don't miss it.

